		{"id":"2967a72b2eab6b07","type":"text","text":"Fire as one of the original human technologies, can be used to destroy whole landscapes or intentionally as \"a paintbrush on the land\" (Brainding Sweetrass pg 360)","x":125,"y":1305,"width":285,"height":200,"color":"6"},
		{"id":"64d8ecafec962289","type":"text","text":"# Planetary Ecosystem\n\nWhat is it?\nThe planetary ecosystem refers to the integrated whole of living and non-living components that make up the Earth system - including the biosphere, atmosphere, hydrosphere, and lithosphere. It encompasses all life forms, ecological communities, nutrient cycles, energy flows and physical processes across the entire planet.\n\nWhy is it important? \nThe planetary ecosystem provides the foundational operating system that allows life to exist and thrive on Earth. All other ecosystems and human systems are ultimately nested within and dependent on the healthy functioning of this overarching system. Regenerating planetary health is crucial for ensuring a continued thriving planetary home for humanity and all species.\n\nWhat are the regeneration objectives?\n- Restoring biodiversity and abundance across all species and ecosystems\n- Rebalancing atmospheric systems and climate patterns \n- Replenishing renewable natural resources like fertile soils, fresh water, etc.\n- Cleaning up accumulated pollutants and toxins\n- Strengthening resilience to disruptions and ability to self-regulate\n\nWhat does life mean in this context?\nIn the planetary ecosystem context, life refers to the interconnected web of living organisms, biogeochemical cycles, and self-sustaining ecological processes that persist and evolve over generations. It is about fostering conditions for continued evolutionary emergence and flourishing of the planetary biosphere as a whole.\n\nHow does technology enable regeneration?\nTechnologies can be a key enabler of planetary regeneration by providing better awareness, analytics, and interventions - if developed and applied with ecosystems principles in mind:\n\n- Monitoring and modeling tools to understand complex planetary dynamics\n- Science and biotech to restore depleted species, ecosystems, nutrients\n- Remediation technologies to clean up pollution and toxins  \n- Sustainable production methods aligned with ecological cycles\n- Systems interventions to rebalance atmospheric systems\n\nWhat technologies are enabling regeneration?\nSome examples of technologies already contributing to regenerative aims:\n\n- Earth observation satellites and IoT sensor networks\n- Ecological data platforms and simulation models\n- Precision ecology tools like habitat mapping \n- Permaculture and regenerative agriculture methods\n- Biochar, mycorestoration, and other soil remediation tech\n- Rewilding projects like Rewilding Europe\n- Decentralized environmental governance platforms\n- Indigenous knowledge systems and traditional practices","x":440,"y":520,"width":708,"height":1360},
		{"id":"8cc50794f3e835e5","type":"text","text":"\"Dumb cities\": using nature itself as an automation technology ","x":1240,"y":1250,"width":250,"height":110,"color":"6"},
		{"id":"dbcea4a04db720f8","type":"text","text":"# Economic Ecosystems \n\nWhat is it?\nAn economic ecosystem refers to the interconnected systems of production, distribution, consumption, and recycling/reuse of goods and services within a specific context (e.g. a country, region, city). It includes the economic activities, resource flows, labor forces, market dynamics, and governing policies and institutions that shape how an economy functions.\n\nWhy is it important?\nEconomic systems are a primary driver of human activity and environmental impacts in the current era. Our prevailing extractive economic model is depleting natural capital and ecosystems at an unsustainable rate. Regenerating economic ecosystems is crucial for realigning human systems with ecological boundaries and cultivating economies that renew and nourish the natural world.\n\nWhat are the regeneration objectives?\n- Transitioning to circular economic models based on renewable energy/materials \n- Internalizing environmental/social costs currently treated as externalities\n- Decoupling economic activity from emissions, waste, and ecological degradation\n- Redefining economic success around health, wellbeing and ecosystem regeneration\n- Balancing local self-reliance and resilience with global efficiency\n\nWhat does life mean in this context? \nLife in an economic context means the ability to continuously renew and replenish the inputs that sustain all economic activity - natural resources, human labor, and shared community wealth. A regenerative economy works to enhance the vitality of the larger ecological-cultural ecosystems it is embedded within.\n\nHow does technology enable regeneration?\nTechnologies can help transform linear economic models to close-looped, regenerative systems by:\n\n- Enabling comprehensive tracking and optimization of resource flows\n- Powering circular production methods recycling waste into inputs\n- Facilitating decentralized production, sharing and localized economic webs\n- Automating laborious processes while prioritizing meaningful human roles\n- Creating new incentive models aligned with regenerative aims\n\nWhat technologies are enabling regeneration? \n- Industrial ecology mapping tools and material tracking databases\n- Renewable energy and storage technologies like solar/wind/batteries\n- Closed-loop manufacturing systems like 3D printing and biofabrication\n- Product lifecycle optimization through IoT, AI and digital twins\n- Sharing economy platforms for mobility, housing, workplace, etc. \n- Blockchain-enabled systems for provenance, voting, resource coordination\n- Alternative financing models like impact investing, community currencies\n","x":2612,"y":520,"width":708,"height":1280},
		{"id":"e99bb723f117f492","type":"text","text":"More about money: how diverse currencies enable regeneration, money as a sacred ritual, money as how we take care of each other on a mass scale","x":2332,"y":1280,"width":250,"height":180,"color":"6"},
