		{"id":"b2a08c31a889f5c3","type":"text","text":"Core institutions like schools, health, family","x":3396,"y":1040,"width":250,"height":60,"color":"6"},
		{"id":"bed48a16d9905d74","type":"text","text":"# Social Ecosystems\n\nWhat is it?\nSocial ecosystems refer to the interwoven formal and informal institutions, norms, relationships and power structures that shape human cooperation and collective behavior within a given community or context. This includes codified institutions like governments, legal systems, and economic entities as well as unwritten cultural customs, traditions, rituals and practices. \n\nWhy is it important?\nInstitutions fundamentally define how societies function - establishing the \"rules of the game\" for decision-making, resource distribution, expression of values and identities, and coordination of human activities. Regenerating social ecosystems is critical for transitioning cultures and communities to sustainable and life-affirming models of human organization and togetherness.\n\nWhat are the regeneration objectives?\n- Transitioning hierarchical power structures to decentralized, participatory models\n- Redefining institutions around ecological values and life-centric worldviews \n- Updating ownership, governance and economic institutions \n- Reviving indigenous wisdom and earth-honoring traditions/practices\n- Fostering cohesion and sense of collective belonging attuned to place\n\nWhat does life mean in this context?\nIn a social context, life refers to the vitality, creativity and continual evolutionary emergence of human cultures and communities. It involves nurturing the diverse expressions of human potential and identity while strengthening the interpersonal bonds and shared stewardship that allow cooperative systems to self-organize and thrive.\n\nHow does technology enable regeneration?\nTechnologies can help redesign antiquated social institutions and catalyze new models by:\n\n- Enabling more participatory decision-making and decentralized governance \n- Creating new mechanisms for collective value creation and shared ownership\n- Enhancing transparency and accountability in power structures\n- Allowing more community-centric economic coordination and provision of public goods\n- Amplifying diverse voices and facilitating intercultural exchange \n\nWhat technologies are enabling regeneration?\n- Blockchain and crypto for decentralized governance, ownership and value\n- Open data, analytics and collective intelligence platforms  \n- Digital community currencies and mutual credit systems\n- Civic technology apps enabling public participation in governance\n- Online spaces for community healing, storytelling and ritual practices\n- Tools for indigenous language preservation and knowledge sharing\n\nTechnologies must be shaped by regenerative values from the start - not reinforcing extractive models of ownership, power hierarchy and social fragmentation. Transparency and collective agency should be prioritized over opaque, centralized control.","x":3680,"y":520,"width":708,"height":1280},
		{"id":"5a4ca953db26e9f1","type":"text","text":"Businesses/cooperatives: technologies for implementing communal benefit institutions like the Cleveland model","x":3396,"y":1130,"width":250,"height":150,"color":"6"},
