		{"id":"93065b366d1d9967","type":"text","text":"We need knowledge on how to care for the earth & each other","x":4480,"y":1010,"width":250,"height":90,"color":"6"},
		{"id":"5d6a5d62e4cc429a","type":"text","text":"# Knowledge Ecosystems\nWhat is it?\nA knowledge ecosystem refers to the interconnected systems, processes and cultural narratives through which knowledge is created, organized, shared and applied within a community or society. This includes formal educational institutions, research disciplines, media landscapes, as well as informal knowledge commons, oral traditions, and embodied practices of meaning-making.\n\nWhy is it important?\nHow we engage with knowledge fundamentally shapes our individual and collective worldviews, beliefs, values and behaviors. Dominant knowledge paradigms rooted in reductionism and exploitation have contributed to humanity's becoming alienated from nature. Regenerating knowledge ecosystems is vital for resituating human understanding and ways of being in integrated relationship with the web of life.\n\nWhat are the regeneration objectives?\n- Transitioning from reductive to holistic knowledge frameworks\n- Decolonizing and integrating diverse knowledge systems, especially indigenous \n- Prioritizing first-hand, place-based and embodied ways of knowing\n- Cultivating wisdom, understanding and reverence for the larger earth community\n- Nurturing collective intelligence and participatory sensemaking capacities\n\nWhat does life mean in this context?\nIn the context of knowledge, life refers to the continual blossoming and cross-pollination of diverse epistemologies, narratives and wisdom traditions in co-evolutionary relationship with the wider biosphere. It is about cultivating living knowledge systems rooted in direct experience, ever-evolving and complexifying in synchrony with the emergence of life itself.\n\nHow does technology enable regeneration?\nTechnologies can assist in regenerating holistic knowledge ecosystems by:\n\n- Providing inclusive knowledge sharing and contextual learning platforms\n- Enabling multi-modal multimedia narratives and art-based sensemaking \n- Amplifying marginalized voices and traditional ecological knowledge\n- Augmenting individual and collective intelligence through interactive tools\n- Creating immersive experiences deepening human's felt relationship with nature\n\nWhat technologies are enabling regeneration?\n- Open knowledge commons, wikis and collaborative databases\n- AI assistants and intelligent tutoring systems contextualized for wisdom\n- Virtual reality/augmented reality for experiential eco-education  \n- Multimedia storytelling platforms integrating art, music, animation\n- Data visualization and embodied sensemaking interfaces \n- Decentralized community learning platforms and skill Web3 credentialing\n\nThe key is developing technologies explicitly guided by life-affriming principles and rooted in direct engagement with the rhythms and patterns of the living world they are nested within.","x":4770,"y":520,"width":708,"height":1280},
		{"id":"4e89ef2ebd8cd025","type":"text","text":"Localized computer chips","x":2332,"y":2200,"width":250,"height":60,"color":"6"},
		{"id":"660d811608c7a425","type":"text","text":"What is so great about craft? A handmade shoe still uses technology: it's that it's made with care, made to last, good quality, benefits the people who make it, sense of culture","x":2332,"y":2280,"width":250,"height":200,"color":"6"},
		{"id":"39c9b594de837058","type":"text","text":"\"What if Western Scientists saw plants as their teachers rather than their subjects? Scientists are particularly good at learning about the lives of other species. But while scientists are among those privy to those other intelligences, many seem to believe that the intelligence they access is only their own.\" BS p 346 ","x":4480,"y":1130,"width":250,"height":370,"color":"6"},
		{"id":"92b46a96311fd27a","type":"text","text":"Machine love: algorithms as knowledge tools to make sense of data and guide discovery. Love is born from intentional pursuit of improvement not from monetary extraction.","x":4480,"y":1520,"width":250,"height":240,"color":"6"},
		{"id":"d06e75d7072551b8","type":"text","text":"Regenerative knowledge & sensemaking spaces","x":4480,"y":1770,"width":250,"height":90,"color":"6"},
		{"id":"f1120426f847367f","type":"text","text":"Brands as moral ecosystems","x":5540,"y":900,"width":250,"height":60,"color":"6"},
		{"id":"34f5ee35b17868ce","type":"text","text":"# Moral Ecosystems\n\nWhat is it?\nA moral ecosystem refers to the interconnected web of values, beliefs, ethical frameworks, and spiritual practices that shape individual and collective understandings of right and wrong, good and bad, meaningful and meaningless within a given cultural context. It encompasses both formal moral philosophies and religions as well as the informal norms, customs, and shared narratives that guide day-to-day social behavior.\n\nWhy is it important?\nOur moral ecosystems fundamentally orient our sense of self, purpose, and relationship with the world around us. The dominant moral paradigm of human supremacy and separation from nature has validated ecologically destructive behaviors. Regenerating our moral ecosystems is essential for realigning human values and lifestyles with the intrinsic worth and sacredness of all life.\n\nWhat are the regeneration objectives?\n- Expanding moral considerability to include the entire community of life\n- Reviving earth-honoring spiritual traditions and practices\n- Cultivating eco-centric values of interdependence, reciprocity and stewardship\n- Redefining the good life around ecological wellbeing and right relationship \n- Socializing bioregional identities and place-based moral obligations\n\nWhat does life mean in this context?\nIn a moral context, life refers to the inherent worth, sentience and creative unfolding of all beings - human and non-human alike. It is about nurturing a felt sense of kinship, reverence and moral responsibility for the flourishing of the entire living world as an interconnected sacred whole.\n\nHow does technology enable regeneration?\nTechnologies can help in evolving our moral ecosystems by:\n\n- Facilitating transformative experiences that shift consciousness and values\n- Enabling new forms of earth-honoring rituals and spiritual practices\n- Amplifying narratives and worldviews that promote eco-centric ethics\n- Providing platforms for inclusive dialogues on moral-existential questions\n- Enabling economic and governance systems aligned with ecological values\n\nWhat technologies are enabling regeneration?\n- Immersive tech (VR/AR) for trans-species empathy and eco-spiritual experiences\n- Multimedia storytelling tools for sharing animistic and regenerative worldviews\n- Blockchain for encoding ecological values into economic and political systems \n- Platforms for philosophical inquiry and collaborative meaning-making\n- Bioregional social networks fostering place-based identities and responsibilities\n\nThe challenge is imbuing the development of technologies themselves with regenerative moral principles, so they don't simply perpetuate human-centric, materialistic value systems. We need a new moral framework for technology itself.","x":5820,"y":520,"width":708,"height":1280},
		{"id":"1ca01c5681909f8a","type":"text","text":"Moral ecosystems shape knowledge ecosystems: teenagers can recognize logos but not leaves","x":5540,"y":980,"width":250,"height":120,"color":"6"},
		{"id":"7704a39a16358a06","type":"text","text":"Stories as moral technologies: \"Mayan stories are considered *ibal*– a precious seeing instrument, or lens, with which to view our sacred relationships \" BS p344","x":5540,"y":1130,"width":250,"height":210,"color":"6"},
		{"id":"a0f67ca66cdee660","type":"text","text":"\"If the western world has an *ibal* it is science... Does science allow us to perceive the sacred in the world, or does it bend light in such a way as to obscure it.\" p 345","x":5540,"y":1360,"width":250,"height":200,"color":"6"},
		{"id":"56596f3cb946f658","type":"text","text":"Rituals as technologies to regenerate our relationship to the land & each other. Indigenous land & harvest practices are closely intertwined with moral technologies of gratefulness, restraint & sufficiency: always take the second one you see, introduce yourself, ask for permission, take only what is given, never more than half, leave a gift. ","x":5540,"y":1570,"width":250,"height":390,"color":"6"},
		{"id":"1562c733cd457d1b","type":"text","text":"[Thiongo wrote in Decolonising the Mind:](https://spectrum.library.concordia.ca/id/eprint/986506/7/Indigenous_Protocol_and_AI_2020.pdf)\n> *The most important domination was the mental universe of the colonized, the control, through culture, of how people perceived themselves and their relationship to the world. Economic and political control can never be complete or effective without mental control. To control a people’s culture is to control their tools of self-definition in relationship to others.*","x":5540,"y":2055,"width":250,"height":570,"color":"6"},
		{"id":"7343177601f8e5fa","type":"text","text":"The Windigo","x":5540,"y":1980,"width":250,"height":60,"color":"6"},
		{"id":"3244e5625c45f7de","type":"text","text":"\"Ceremony focuses attention so that attention becomes intention.\" BS p249","x":5820,"y":1815,"width":250,"height":145,"color":"6"}
	],
	"edges":[]
}